  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Periods of unsettled weather are possible today.\n\nLongwave trough axis overhead of the region this morning will \ngradually shift east through tonight, supporting a ridge of surface \nhigh pressure to gradually build into the eastern Great Lakes. \nOverall, synoptic scale low level cold air advection will continue \nthrough today. While primarily fair weather is expected through \ntonight, periodic lake effect rain showers are possible over and \ndownwind of Lake America, this morning through this evening. Lake \neffect rain showers will focus east of the lake, across Oswego \nCounty and toward the Tug Hill Plateau. Showers will taper off this \nevening as the boundary layer stabilizes and dry air from the ridge \nadvects into the region. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...The remnants of Hurricane Isaias will bring a \nsoaking rain to the region Sunday and Sunday Night.\n\nRidging at the surface and aloft will gradually exit east across \nwestern and north central New York as the remnants of Hurricane of \nIsaias becomes vertically-stacked as it passes through the trough \naloft into the mid-Atlantic Seaboard region. Isolated rain showers \nwill be possible after midnight Saturday night, especially across \nthe western Southern Tier, due to moist isentropic lift aloft \ngenerated by the low pressure system. \n\nHeading into Sunday, the low will  pass northeastward from the lower \nOhio Valley toward the central Appalachians, while the trough axis \nnears the forecast area, supporting widespread steady rain (heavy at \ntimes), to overspread the region from south to north as isentropic \nlift aloft continues across the region and taps into the plume of \nmoisture originating from the southern Gulf Stream.\n\nThe trough axis aloft will then pass from the Great Lakes to the \nAppalachians and further toward the Canadian Maritimes Sunday night \nthrough Monday. Meanwhile the vertically stacked surface low will \ndissipate over the Ohio Valley Sunday, while a new surface low \ndevelops over the Delmarva Peninsula and deepen while moving \nnortheastward toward southern New England Monday. Additionally \nMonday, a surface ridge of high pressure will build across the \neastern Great Lakes. Overall this will act to taper the rain from \nwest to east Sunday night into early Monday morning, with \npredominantly dry weather expected for the remainder of Monday.\n\nCurrent forecasted rainfall amounts for Sunday through Sunday night \nrange from a quarter of an inch to near a half of an inch, with the \nhigher amounts occurring across southern New York.",
